 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "Why would primes care where zeros are?",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 2.925
  },
  {
   "text": "Because of an exact formula, the explicit formula,",
   "start": 2.925,
   "end": 5.975
  },
  {
   "text": "from Riemann's paper, proved in this form by von Mangoldt.",
   "start": 5.975,
   "end": 9.75
  },
  {
   "text": "Weight every prime power by the log of its prime,",
   "start": 9.75,
   "end": 12.325
  },
  {
   "text": "and count: that's ψ(x), a cleaner staircase.",
   "start": 12.325,
   "end": 16.0
  },
  {
   "text": "The formula says psi of x is x, minus one wave for every zero.",
   "start": 16.0,
   "end": 20.5
  },
  {
   "text": "A zero at one half plus i gamma adds a wave that oscillates with frequency gamma in",
   "start": 21.45,
   "end": 26.275
  },
  {
   "text": "log x.",
   "start": 26.275,
   "end": 26.912
  },
  {
   "text": "Start with the smooth guess, x.",
   "start": 26.912,
   "end": 28.762
  },
  {
   "text": "Add the first wave.",
   "start": 28.762,
   "end": 30.3
  },
  {
   "text": "Ten.",
   "start": 30.3,
   "end": 31.037
  },
  {
   "text": "A hundred.",
   "start": 31.037,
   "end": 31.938
  },
  {
   "text": "A thousand.",
   "start": 31.938,
   "end": 33.075
  },
  {
   "text": "The prime staircase comes out of pure waves.",
   "start": 33.075,
   "end": 36.037
  },
  {
   "text": "The zeros are the frequencies; the primes are the signal.",
   "start": 36.037,
   "end": 39.7
  }
 ],
